 **API threading.** Add `timeoutMs?: number` to `PromptOptions` in `opencode-runner.ts`, versus a fifth positional argument to `runPrompt`, versus passing seconds at the API boundary. The positional argument is rejected: it worsens every call site (`runPrompt(client, {}, schema, prompt, 30)` reads poorly) and churns all test call sites for no gain, while `PromptOptions` is the established place for per-run knobs (`agent`, `title`). A `timeoutSeconds` field at the API boundary is rejected because the repository's internal convention is milliseconds (`stageTimeoutMs`, `POLL_TIMEOUT_MS`, A10); seconds are reserved for the human-facing CLI surface (A6).
 
-**Value semantics for `0`, negative, fractional, and huge inputs.** Reject everything except integer seconds in `1..3_600`, versus libpq/curl/coreutils-style "`0` means wait forever". Waiting forever is rejected: this CLI exists as a bounded connectivity/auth smoke test whose purpose is defeated by an unbounded wait, and the task pins the bounded 30 s as the default (A14). Fractional seconds are rejected because the prior-art human-facing interfaces are integer seconds (A6, libpq explicitly "decimal integer") and sub-second polling granularity is meaningless against a 250 ms poll interval; coreutils' floating-point durations are the outlier and are tied to its unit-suffix syntax we are not adopting. The upper bound is a **product decision** (A16), not an evidence-backed limit, and is sized from an abuse standpoint as much as a typo-guard one: with the interval fixed at 250 ms, a valid wait issues roughly `timeoutMs / 250` `message.list` requests — ~121 at the 30 s default, ~14 400 at the 3 600 s cap — so the cap also bounds request pressure and the recursion's pending-promise depth against whatever `OPENCODE_BASE_URL` names. An 86 400 s bound (2880× the default, ~345 600 polls) was considered and judged too large a self-inflicted DoS surface for a smoke test; no upper bound at all was considered (the wall-clock deadline makes huge values technically safe, A9) and rejected because a mistyped unit would silently hammer a server. If long timeouts ever become normal practice, making the poll interval adaptive is the obvious follow-up — the fixed interval is the knob that becomes load-relevant at large `--timeout` values.
